'''Bintu Cavall Rovira''' (born 22 March 1976) is a Balboan former professional footballer and current head coach of the [[Balboa women's national football team]]. As a player, she was a [[Forward (association football)|striker]] widely regarded as one of the two greatest players in the history of Balboan women's football, alongside her longtime rival and contemporary [[Adama Sow]]. Cavall is the older sister of [[Nèstir Cavall]], the greatest Balboan male footballer of all time, making the Cavall family the most prominent footballing dynasty in Balboan sporting history.

== Early life ==

Cavall was born in the Costa Nord district of [[Sant Cristòfor]], Balboa, to a Volisanian-Balboan father, Sergi Cavall, a set builder for the Sant Cristòfor film studios, and a Black Balboan mother, Adama Rovira, a chef at a restaurant in [[Original (ciutat)|Original]], Sant Cristòfor. She is the eldest of three children; her younger siblings are [[Oriol Cavall]] and [[Nèstir Cavall]], the latter of whom would become the most celebrated male footballer in Balboan history.

Cavall grew up in the same Sant Cristòfor football culture that shaped her brother, but her path to the professional game was more difficult. Women's football in Balboa in the 1980s and 1990s was underfunded, marginalised, and largely invisible compared to the men's La Lliga. The [[Federació Balboana de Futbol]] (FBF) did not formally recognise the women's game until 1991, and the first national women's league — the [[Lliga Femenina]] — was not established until 1993. Cavall, like most of her generation of women's players, learned the game on the same vacant lots and informal pitches that had produced the [[Lliga de les Graderies|Terraces League]] tradition, playing with boys and men who recognised her talent long before the institutional structures did.

She joined the [[Unió Esportiva del Mercat]] women's team at the age of eighteen, in 1994, the year the club established its women's section — one of the first La Lliga clubs to do so.

== Playing career ==

=== Unió Esportiva del Mercat (1994–2008) ===

Cavall spent the majority of her club career at [[Unió Esportiva del Mercat]], the club of Sant Cristòfor's historic Black quarter, where she became the most prolific striker in the [[Lliga Femenina]]'s early history. In fourteen seasons at the Mercat, she scored 134 goals in 168 appearances, won the Lliga Femenina four times (1997, 2000, 2003, 2006), and established the club as the dominant force in the women's game.

Her playing style was characterised by explosive pace, physical strength, and a ruthless finishing instinct that drew comparisons to the great Terraces League forwards. Where her brother Nèstir's game was defined by creativity and flair, Bintu's was defined by directness and power — a contrast that the Balboan sporting press never tired of analysing.

=== Club Esportiu Sant Bart (2008–2012) ===

In 2008, at the age of 32, Cavall moved to [[Club Esportiu Sant Bart]] (women), the women's section of the most successful club in Balboan football. The transfer was controversial — the rivalry between the Mercat and Sant Bart carried the full weight of the corridor–interior divide, and Cavall's decision to leave the Black quarter's club for the interior's powerhouse was seen by some Mercat supporters as a betrayal. Cavall defended the move as a desire to test herself in a new environment and to help develop the women's game in the interior, where it lagged behind the corridor.

She played four seasons at Sant Bart, winning two more Lliga Femenina titles (2009, 2011) and scoring 47 goals in 82 appearances. Her presence at Sant Bart elevated the profile of the women's game in the interior and contributed to the rapid improvement of the club's youth development programme.

=== International career ===

Cavall made her debut for the [[Balboa women's national football team]] in 1995, at the age of nineteen, and remained an integral part of the squad for seventeen years. She earned 97 caps and scored 72 goals — the latter a record that stood until 2022, when it was surpassed by [[Mariama Dia]].

The defining rivalry of Cavall's international career was with [[Adama Sow]], the [[Club Esportiu Haimêng]] striker who was the other titan of Balboan women's football. Cavall and Sow — the '''Dues Dives''' (The Two Goddesses), as the Balboan press dubbed them — dominated the women's game for a decade and a half, their rivalry dividing the country along the same lines as the men's [[Clàssic de l'Istme]]: the Mercat's Black quarter identity against Haimêng's Zong-heritage culture, the corridor's working-class passion against the Fosian community's sporting tradition.

Cavall and Sow faced each other in six Lliga Femenina title deciders and numerous international tournaments. Their on-field rivalry was fierce but respectful; off the field, they maintained a cordial relationship that occasionally saw them collaborate on advocacy for women's football in Balboa. The '''Derbi Femení''' — the Mercat vs. Haimêng women's fixture — became the most attended and most anticipated match in the Lliga Femenina calendar, and the two players' mutual excellence elevated the entire women's game.

Cavall retired from international football in 2012, at the age of 36, following the [[2012 Abyalan Women's Championship]]. Her final match — a 2–1 victory over [[Ayorin]] in which she scored the winning goal — was played at the [[Camp del Mercat]], the ground where she had spent the majority of her career, and she received a standing ovation from both sets of supporters.

== Coaching career ==

=== Balboa women's national team (2020–present) ===

Cavall was appointed head coach of the [[Balboa women's national football team]] in 2020, succeeding [[Fatoumata Keita]]. Her appointment was widely welcomed — the Cavall name carries enormous weight in Balboan football, and Bintu's own playing career gave her immediate credibility with the squad.

Under Cavall's management, the Balboa women's team has adopted a more aggressive, forward-pressing style that reflects her own playing philosophy. The team reached the semi-finals of the [[2024 Abyalan Women's Championship]] — the nation's best result in the competition — and has qualified for the [[2027 AFA Women's World Cup]].

Cavall has been a vocal advocate for investment in women's football infrastructure, equal pay for women's players, and the expansion of the Lliga Femenina to a fully professional league. Her public profile — as a Cavall, as a former great of the women's game, and as a charismatic and outspoken figure — has made her one of the most influential voices in Balboan sporting culture.

== Personal life ==

Cavall's partner is Shen Wei-Lin, a Zong-Balboan multimedia artist whose installations and video work explore identity, displacement, and the Zong diaspora in Sant Cristòfor. The couple have been together since 2005 and have one child together, a son, Kèma Cavall-Shen (born 2010). Cavall also has an older son, [[Liang Cavall]] (born 2005), from a previous relationship, who plays as a forward for [[Club Esportiu Sant Bart]] in the men's La Lliga.

Cavall's relationship with her brother [[Nèstir Cavall]] is close and well-documented. The two have frequently spoken about their shared football upbringing and the competitive dynamic that drove both of them to excel. In a 2023 interview, Nèstir said of his sister: "Bintu was the best striker in the family. I just got the attention because I played in the men's league. If the women's game had had the same platform when we were growing up, she would have been the global name, not me."

